<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Evaluating Definite integrals using integration by parts</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Evaluate \( \displaystyle \int_{@lower_limit@}^{@upper_limit@} ({@func_var_u@})\cdot {@func_var_dv@} dx\)</p>
<p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]][[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked solution</h3>
<p>Evaluate \( \displaystyle \int_{@lower_limit@}^{@upper_limit@} ({@func_var_u@})\cdot {@func_var_dv@} dx\)</p>
<p> This is a definite integral, but first, we need to compute it like any other indefinite integral. Then substitute the limits of integration later towards the end.</p>
<p>The first step here is to pick \(u\) and \(dv\) so that when we compute \(du\) and \(v\) </p>
<p>\(u =  \displaystyle {@func_var_u@}\), \( \displaystyle dv ={@func_var_dv@} dx\).</p>
<p>\ \displaystyle (du = {@diff_u@} dx\), \( \displaystyle v= {@int_v@}\)</p>
<p> We need to plug in the above and use the integration-by-parts formula as follows:</p>
<p>\( \displaystyle \int_{@lower_limit@}^{@upper_limit@} udv = \displaystyle \left[ u \cdot v \right]_{@lower_limit@}^{@upper_limit@} - \int_{@lower_limit@}^{@upper_limit@} vdu\)<br></p>
<p>\( \begin{align}
 \displaystyle \int_{@lower_limit@}^{@upper_limit@}({@func_var_u@})\cdot {@func_var_dv@} dx & = \left[({@func_var_u@})\cdot({@int_v@})\right]_{@lower_limit@}^{@upper_limit@} - \displaystyle \int_{@lower_limit@}^{@upper_limit@}({@int_v@}){@diff_u@} \, dx\\
& = \left[({@func_var_u@})\cdot({@int_v@})\right]_{@lower_limit@}^{@upper_limit@} - {@int(int_v*diff_u,x,lower_limit,upper_limit)@} \\
& = \left({@at(func_var_u*int_v,[x=upper_limit])@}\right) - \left({@at(func_var_u*int_v,[x=lower_limit])@} \right) - {@int(int_v*diff_u,x,lower_limit,upper_limit)@} \\
& = {@ta@}
\end{align} \)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
